    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T17:43:46Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Data Science Education in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore documented Iranian data science courses, their technical foundations, and the limits of available evidence on participation and outcomes."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "documented-pathways",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different routes into working with data",
        "content": "Iranian examples illustrate several routes into data science rather than a single standardized educational pathway. The Foundations of Data Science website identifies its course as undergraduate teaching in the Department of Electrical Engineering at Sharif University of Technology. Its scope extends from databases, visualization, probability, and statistics to machine learning and the practical movement of data through processing and monitoring. This places data science within an engineering curriculum, rather than presenting it solely as training in a programming language. [s1]\n\nCommercial training offers another configuration. Sematec's published comprehensive data science syllabus combines relational databases, SQL, Power BI, and Python with later analytical topics. SQL is a language for querying structured databases; Power BI is Microsoft's reporting and visualization software. Their inclusion shows why a course carrying the data science label may devote substantial attention to retrieving, organizing, and communicating data before advanced modeling. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "curriculum-distinctions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Read the curriculum behind the title",
        "content": "These examples emphasize different parts of the field. Sharif's course description includes deep learning, transformers, and generative models alongside statistical foundations. Sematec's syllabus gives detailed attention to database queries, report preparation, and dashboards. The distinction matters: a program centered on organizational reporting and a course centered on statistical learning can share tools while developing different capabilities. This is an editorial comparison of the published curricula, not a ranking of their quality. [s1][s2]\n\nQuera's data analysis bootcamp provides a further example of applied learning. Its published introductory topics include Python, NumPy, Pandas, visualization, and statistics. NumPy supports numerical computation, while Pandas helps organize and manipulate tabular data. The provider expects basic Python knowledge and elementary mathematics, and describes recorded instruction combined with online mentoring and team projects. This advertised format assumes preparation before the course begins. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "assessment-and-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the evidence establishes",
        "content": "The inspected materials establish intended subject coverage and teaching arrangements. They do not demonstrate how consistently students master those subjects. Sematec lists final projects within its syllabus, while Quera advertises practical exercises and collaborative projects. Such assessment opportunities are relevant evidence about course design; they are not independently verified evidence of graduate competence or hiring success. [s2][s3]\n\nFor international readers, the useful conclusion is therefore specific: documented Iranian provision connects mathematical and computational learning with database work and practical analysis. A fuller account still requires verified degree regulations, participation statistics, and independent evidence about learning outcomes. The examples here should not be treated as a census of Iranian institutions or as proof that the same curriculum is available across the country.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
