    "name": "Technology Bootcamps",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/education/technology-education/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iranian technology bootcamps include intensive programs advertised around practical exercises, mentoring, and collaborative projects. Inspected Quera materials illustrate this format, while leaving national participation and independently verified employment outcomes unresolved.",
    "tags": ["Bootcamps", "Technology training", "Career development", "Online learning", "Project-based learning"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T17:43:46Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T17:43:46Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Technology Bootcamps in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Examine documented Iranian bootcamp formats, prerequisites, mentoring, and the distinction between advertised career preparation and verified outcomes."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "format",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An intensive approach to practical training",
        "content": "Quera, an Iranian programming and technology education platform, presents its bootcamps as intensive preparation for employment. Its catalog includes programs covering software development and data-related work, alongside newer AI-oriented subjects. Team projects recur across the advertised descriptions. This provides a documented example of how one provider organizes practical technology training; it does not establish a uniform national bootcamp model. [s1]\n\nThe catalog also separates programs advertised for registration from those described as already running. That distinction matters when reading an educational website: the existence of a course page does not establish that applications remain open. Cohort availability is changeable, and the undated live catalog inspected for this article cannot reconstruct precisely what was available at the editorial cutoff. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "learning-arrangements",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What participation can involve",
        "content": "Quera's data analysis page describes a combination of recorded teaching, online support, exercises, and team projects. It expects basic Python proficiency, elementary mathematics, and willingness to collaborate. Its frequently asked questions distinguish introductory programs from more advanced programs requiring prior knowledge. The word bootcamp therefore does not, by itself, mean a course is suitable for a complete beginner. [s2]\n\nThe same page describes mentoring and online question sessions. These arrangements make the advertised experience different from simply watching a collection of videos: participants are expected to practice and interact. They remain provider descriptions, however. This research did not inspect classroom delivery, assess submitted work, or verify how much individual feedback participants actually received. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "access-and-outcomes",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Access, continuity, and career claims",
        "content": "Connectivity appears explicitly in Quera's operational planning. Its catalog says that some educational resources can remain accessible through domestic Internet infrastructure during international connectivity restrictions. This is evidence of an advertised contingency arrangement, not proof that every service or learning activity would continue uninterrupted. It nevertheless shows why delivery infrastructure belongs in an account of online technical education, alongside the syllabus. [s1]\n\nCareer preparation also needs careful interpretation. An advertised certificate or an opportunity to build projects does not independently establish qualification recognition, a job offer, or a salary outcome. The data analysis page supports claims about promised learning arrangements, but this review found no audited cohort study establishing their employment effects. [s2]\n\nA fuller national picture would compare multiple providers and examine admissions, accessibility, completion, and subsequent employment using consistent definitions. Until that evidence is available, these materials are best read as a concrete case study of advertised provision. They support an account of course design and expectations, while leaving the size and effectiveness of Iran's bootcamp sector open to further research.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
